# Registration of the control cells added after review (2026-09-30)

Status: registered before any of these cells ran on a model (code in commits `47d5b26`, `db0029d`,
`0180213`; the dated rows in `prereg/decisions.md` of 2026-09-30 are the authoritative record). The
executed scope was reduced before the first run to worlds 1000-1009 and the cells listed in the last
of those rows; predictions that refer to cells that were not run are withdrawn there.

What is registered is of two kinds, kept apart throughout:

- **Facts computed offline from the worlds** (section 1). The keys of the oracle cell, the scope cell
  (on definition items) and the source-epoch cell are functions of the world, so their detection is
  known before any model runs. They are audited with `python -m memshift.analysis.e1_keys` and are not
  predictions. A run whose rates for these keys differ from the audit has a bug and is void.
- **Predictions** (section 3), each with a threshold that can be missed and an analysis.

## 0. What was built

| Cell or arm | Token in `--cells` | Name in the outputs | New model requests |
|---|---|---|---|
| Oracle key | `D0B1_gold`, `D1B1_gold` | `holdmem_D{0,1}B1_gold` | writer: 6 in total over 20 worlds and 3 branches |
| Refresh without invalidation | `D0B0_refresh`, `D1B0_refresh` | `holdmem_D{0,1}B0_refresh` | none |
| Template presentation of D=0 | `D0tB0`, `D0tB1_source` | `holdmem_D0tB0`, `holdmem_D0tB1_source` | none |
| Retraction-aware programs | `D1sB1_source_ra`, `D1sB0_ra` | `holdmem_D1sB1_source_ra`, `holdmem_D1sB0_ra` | program: 51 per world |
| Scope-aware key | `D0B1_scope`, `D1B1_scope` | `holdmem_D{0,1}B1_scope` | writer: 7 in total over 20 worlds and 3 branches |
| Query-time view | `zero_Dm` | `zero_Dm` | none (program requests are the D1s ones) |
| Thinking writer | `D0thinkB0`, `D0thinkB1_source` | `holdmem_D0thinkB0`, `holdmem_D0thinkB1_source` | thinking writer: 60 to 68 (B=1) and 30 to 32 (B=0) per world |

Design notes that the predictions depend on:

1. **Oracle key (`gold`).** The key of a slot at time t is the sorted (object id, revision) list of
   `gold_at(slot, t).deps` plus the definition version. An item is invalidated when its key differs
   from the key at its last derivation, and is re-derived with the request the source-epoch cell would
   send at that checkpoint. It is an oracle (it reads the gold) and is not implementable.
2. **Scope key (`scope`).** For a definition item the key is (definition version, the (object id,
   revision) list of the objects the definition's scope ranges over at t). The range is the executable
   form of `SCOPE_PREDICATES` (`gen/kappa.py: SCOPE_RANGES`, written from the definition text, reads no
   evaluator output); windows that slide with t are part of the range, so the key moves when an object
   enters or leaves a window. Reading "epochs of the sources the predicates range over" literally gives
   the source-epoch key again (the sources are the declared ones) except for `working_hours` v1, so the
   range fingerprint is used. All other items are keyed by their cited clusters, exactly as in
   `B1_cited`. No key reads the writer's value or the D=1 extractor.
3. **Refresh without invalidation (`B0_refresh`).** A B=0 cell that, at each checkpoint, re-derives
   every item whose source-epoch key moved (which is what the B=1 source-epoch cell re-derives, with
   the same full in-scope records and the same request), appends the new item and never invalidates.
   The old derivation stays valid and is served beside the new one.
4. **Template presentation (`D0t`).** D=0 without checker or sidecar. A repaired or new item written
   after the shift is rendered by `render_fallback_sk` from the writer's own typed value and its own
   citations, with the "under the definition in effect" clause and the D=1 provenance phrase
   ("programmatic extraction from ..."), verbatim. Items of the pre-shift snapshot keep the writer's
   statement, so the pre-shift contexts are those of every other cell. Note that D=1 applies the
   template only to items whose check failed; D0t applies it to every item written after the shift.
5. **Retraction-aware programs (`_ra`).** A second program request per item. The function is
   `derive(records, as_of, prior_reads)` and returns `{"value": V, "reads": [ids]}`. The sandbox wraps
   every record: reading a content field of a record whose id is not in `reads` is an error
   ("undeclared read"). Identification fields (`object_id`, `source`, `revision`, `updated`, `cluster`
   and per source the locator fields `name`, `kind`/`title`/`appt_type`, `title`/`project`, `from_addr`,
   `conversation_with`/`contact`, `title`) can be read on any record so that a program can find its
   record without declaring every record it looked at. `prior_reads` is the item's lineage: every id
   any earlier run declared for that item (the layer keeps it, also across a failed run). The prompt
   asks the program to return `None` (for set items too) when an id of `prior_reads` no longer belongs
   to a live record and not to substitute another record. The sandbox does not enforce that rule; the
   layer measures it (`lineage_ok`, per run) and reports it.
6. **Query-time view (`zero_Dm`).** The zero system (BM25 over the live objects the agent may read)
   plus, for the probed item, the value of its D1s program (the same cached request) run on the live
   records at the probe's as-of time, rendered with the D=1 template and the provenance phrase "computed
   by the writer's own program". The view leads the evidence and shares the 4,096-token evidence
   budget (the BM25 evidence is packed into what the view leaves). No derived state is stored.
   Items whose program fails, or whose sources the agent may not read, are served as the zero system
   serves them.
7. **Thinking writer (`D0think`).** D=0. Items of the pre-shift snapshot are written by the normal
   writer (same bytes as every cell). Definition items written after the shift come from a second
   client with `enable_thinking: True`, `max_tokens` 8,192, no JSON schema (a schema that has to open
   with `{` leaves no room to think on a server without a reasoning parser), and the prompt extended by
   one line naming the JSON keys. The body, hence the response-cache key, differs from every other
   request. A reply cut off by the token limit is an unreadable write and is counted.

## 1. Facts computed offline (worlds 1000 to 1019, 12 months; no model)

`python -m memshift.analysis.e1_keys --seeds 1000-1019 --branches src,def,orphan`. "Changed" means the
gold at the checkpoint differs from the gold at t0 (as in `analysis/e1.py`). Item-checkpoints pool the
four checkpoints (1, 2, 4, 8 weeks). Rates are over item-checkpoints.

| Branch | Key | Missed (all classes) | False invalidation: all / definition / rule / free text | Re-derivations per world (all / definition) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| S_src | source epoch | 0.0% | 99.7 / 98.6 / 99.7 / 100.0 % | 190.0 / 23.5 |
| S_src | oracle (gold) | 0.0% | 16.8 / 50.3 / 16.4 / 1.5 % | 38.2 / 18.9 |
| S_src | scope, definition items only | 0.0% | - / 53.1 / - / - % | - / 19.1 |
| S_def | source epoch | 0.0% | 99.3 / 95.7 / 99.3 / 100.0 % | 189.6 / 23.5 |
| S_def | oracle (gold) | 0.0% | 4.3 / 61.3 / 2.9 / 1.1 % | 27.7 / 20.8 |
| S_def | scope, definition items only | 0.0% | - / 64.5 / - / - % | - / 21.5 |
| orphan | source epoch | 0.0% | 100.0 / 100.0 / 100.0 / 100.0 % | 190.4 / 23.6 |
| orphan | oracle (gold) | 0.0% | 7.0 / 49.4 / 3.7 / 1.1 % | 40.3 / 18.7 |
| orphan | scope, definition items only | 0.0% | - / 52.3 / - / - % | - / 19.0 |

Changed / unchanged item-checkpoints: S_src 1,168 / 2,672; S_def 558 / 3,282; orphan 1,767 / 2,073.
Source-epoch false invalidation reproduces the published 99.7 / 99.3 / 100.0%.

Three consequences that change what the brief suggested:

- The oracle key does **not** have false invalidation below 5% in every branch: 16.8% after S_src and
  7.0% on the orphan branch (it is 4.3% after S_def). Gold dependencies include the revision of each
  object, so a revised record with the same value invalidates the item, and on definition items a
  window that slides changes the dependency set while the value often stays. The suggested "< 5%"
  is therefore replaced by the audit above. On definition items false invalidation is about 50 to 61%.
- Definition items are re-derived at 78 to 87% of their checkpoints even under the oracle key (18.7 to
  20.8 of at most 24 per world, against 23.5 for the source-epoch key). Selective and complete binding
  saves almost nothing on the items the D effect lives in; its saving is on the other classes (rule
  items: 344 vs 2,930 re-derivations in 20 worlds after S_src).
- The scope key is almost the oracle on definition items (false invalidation 52 to 65% against 49 to
  61%); with both at zero missed, any Update difference between them and the source-epoch cell on
  definition items is model noise, not detection.

Uncached writer requests of the keys (a re-derivation at a checkpoint where the source-epoch cell did
not re-derive, because the clock moved a window): across 20 worlds and 3 branches, 6 for the oracle
key and 7 for the scope key in total (worlds 1015, 1018, 1019 only, several shared between branches);
0 in seeds 1000 and 1001 in the full replay. Every other writer request of these cells is one the
source-epoch cells sent.

## 2. Common analysis (all predictions below)

- Probes: the discriminative probes of the published E1 runs (all items whose gold changed since t0
  plus an equal-size seeded sample of unchanged items, selected from gold only), so every system is
  asked the same questions in every world, branch and checkpoint; `--discriminative` is required.
- Worlds 1000 to 1019 (think arm: 1000 to 1009); branches S_src, S_def, orphan; checkpoints 1, 2, 4, 8
  weeks after the fork, pooled. Anchor cells (`D0B0`, `D0B1_source`, `D1B1_source`, `D1sB1_source`,
  `zero`) come from the published runs on the same worlds (same code, same writer and reader cache).
- Unit: per-item paired difference (same fact id at the same checkpoint), clustered by world, 95% world
  bootstrap interval with 2,000 draws and seed 996 (`scoring.stats`). Metrics: Update and Stability
  (ContractValid on changed and on unchanged asked probes), Missed and False invalidation, repair
  correctness (ContractValid given the item was repaired at that checkpoint; mechanical variant: the
  repaired value equals the gold). Classes as in `analysis.e1.ITEM_CLASSES`: definition (`k.`), rule
  (structured fields), free text.
- A prediction "holds" exactly as its threshold says; all contrasts are reported, including those that
  fail. No multiplicity correction (as v1, v2). Anything not listed is exploratory and labelled so.
- Implementation checks (not predictions): the pre-shift hash assertion must pass for every cell of
  a run (it covers the new cells); the run's Missed and False invalidation for the gold cells and for the
  scope cells on definition items must equal section 1; the scope cells' non-definition items must
  equal the `B1_cited` cell's rates.

## 3. Predictions

### 3.1 Oracle key (`B1_gold`)

Question: does selective and complete binding keep the benefit of refreshing everything?

- **P-G1** (non-inferiority on Update, all items). For D in {0,1} and each of S_src, S_def, orphan,
  `Update(B1_gold) - Update(B1_source) >= -0.02` (point estimate). Holds if true in all six contrasts;
  reported with the pooled interval (holds strongly if its lower bound is above -0.04).
- **P-G2** (definition items). `|Update(B1_gold) - Update(B1_source)| <= 0.03` on definition items in
  each of the six (D, branch) contrasts. Rationale: the audit shows both keys re-derive the same items
  at almost the same checkpoints, so the oracle should neither help nor hurt there. Fails if the oracle
  is better or worse than the refresh on these items by more than 3 pp.
- **P-G3** (regressions). `Stability(B1_gold) - Stability(B1_source) >= 0` in at least five of the six
  (D, branch) contrasts: fewer re-derivations leave fewer items to regress (the net effect of binding
  that the review found missing).
- Consequence. P-G1 true: the benefit of refreshing everything is recoverable with dependency
  tracking, and what the paper called "binding" is paying for re-derivation it does not need. P-G1
  false: complete dependency tracking loses something the refresh has (it hides re-derivations that fix
  a stale value whose dependencies did not change, for instance a value written from an earlier
  record). Either is reported as measured.

### 3.2 Refresh without invalidation (`B0_refresh`)

Question: is the B effect "a fresh derivation is added" or "the stale one is removed"?

- **P-R1** (ordering, all items). For each D and branch,
  `Update(B0) - 0.01 <= Update(B0_refresh) <= Update(B1_source) + 0.01` (point estimates). Holds if true
  in at least five of the six.
- **P-R2** (closer to B=1, definition items, D=1). In S_src and S_def,
  `(Update(B0_refresh) - Update(B0)) / (Update(B1_source) - Update(B0)) >= 0.5` (ratio of pooled
  means; not evaluated if the denominator is below 0.05). Holds if true in both.
- Orphan, definition and other classes: reported, no threshold (old and new derivations coexist, so the
  direction is not obvious).
- Consequence. P-R2 true: most of the B effect on definition items is adding the fresh derivation; the
  binding's removal of the stale one matters little there. False: removal matters.

### 3.3 Template presentation (`D0t`)

Question: is the D effect a matter of value, or of the template it is shown in after the shift?

- **P-T1** (primary). `|Update(D0tB{0,1}) - Update(D0B{0,1})| <= 0.03` on definition items, for B in
  {0,1} and each of the three branches (six contrasts), as point estimates. Power note: a definition
  item contributes about 50 changed item-checkpoints per world over the three branches, so a 95%
  interval narrower than plus or minus 0.03 is unlikely; the prediction is on the point estimates and
  the intervals are reported.
- **P-T2** (share of the D effect that is presentation). At B=1 in S_src and S_def on definition items,
  `(Update(D0t) - Update(D0)) / (Update(D1) - Update(D0)) <= 0.15`.
- **P-T3** (all items). `|Update(D0t) - Update(D0)| <= 0.03` for B in {0,1} and each branch.
- Consequence. All three true: the D effect on Update is value, not presentation, and "D = check +
  executable re-derivation + template" can say which part matters. P-T1 false with
  `Update(D0t) - Update(D0) >= 0.10` on definition items: most of the D effect there is presentation, and
  the paper's execution claim must be restated.

### 3.4 Retraction-aware programs (`D1sB1_source_ra`, `D1sB0_ra`)

Question: does design rule 3 (a derivation that declares its inputs and is withdrawn with them) repair
the retraction failure of the writer's own programs (-7.8 pp on the orphan branch)?

- **P-M1** (primary, orphan branch, B=1, source epoch). (a) `RepairCorrectness(D1sB1_source_ra) -
  RepairCorrectness(D0B1_source) >= 0` (point estimate; for D_m the published value is -7.8 pp); (b)
  `RepairCorrectness(D1sB1_source_ra) - RepairCorrectness(D1sB1_source)` has a 95% interval above 0.
  Holds if both.
- **P-M2** (no cost where nothing is retracted). On S_src and S_def, `Update(D1sB1_source_ra) -
  Update(D1sB1_source) >= -0.03` on all items (point estimate).
- **P-M3** (executability, a validity condition). The share of `_ra` programs that run (ok) at t0, over
  all items, is at least 0.8 times that of the D1s programs. If it is lower, the `_ra` result is a
  result about prompt compliance (as the first smoke of block C was) and is reported as inconclusive
  for rule 3; P-M1 and P-M2 are then not judged.
- Measured and reported (no threshold): the share of runs that returned a value although a prior read
  was gone (`lineage_ok` false), the share that failed by an undeclared read, by class and branch; and
  the over-retraction of definition items over tasks on the orphan branch (a program that reads every
  task must declare every task, so a deleted task withdraws it; `active_project` and `overdue_task`
  are expected to lose there, as rule 3 over-retracts multi-witness items).
- B=0 counterpart: reported, no threshold. A B=0 layer never re-derives an item whose sources have no
  new record, so a deleted object is repaired only when a delta arrives.

### 3.5 Scope-aware key (`B1_scope`)

Question: does a key that ranges over what the definition ranges over (selective and scope-complete)
recover the refresh-everything benefit on definition items?

- **P-K1**. On definition items, `Update(B1_scope) - Update(B1_source) >= -0.02` for D in {0,1} and
  each branch (six contrasts, point estimates).
- **Audit-checked, not predicted:** missed on definition items is 0.0% (section 1, bound by the
  prediction "at most 2%"), and false invalidation on definition items is 52.3 to 64.5% against 95.7
  to 100.0% (at most 0.7 times the source-epoch rate in every branch).
- **P-K2** (what the scope key cannot do). On the non-definition items the scope cell is the cited
  cell; `|Update(B1_scope) - Update(B1_cited)| <= 0.01` on those items, and the scope cell's overall
  missed rate is above 0.05 (the cited key misses 15 to 21%). This is stated so that the scope result
  is not read as a result for all items.
- Consequence. P-K1 true together with P-G2: selective and complete binding on definition items is as
  good as the refresh, at 80 to 91% of its re-derivations on those items; the saving is small because
  definitions over time windows change at almost every checkpoint.

### 3.6 Query-time view (`zero_Dm`)

Question: when the derived item is computed at read time from live records, is there nothing to bind
and nothing lost?

- **P-Z1** (definition items, primary). `Update(zero_Dm) - Update(zero) >= 0.30` in S_src and S_def
  (each a point estimate, pooled lower bound above 0.20).
- **P-Z2** (other items). On rule items (structured fields), `Update(zero_Dm) - Update(zero) >= 0` in S_src
  and S_def; on free-text items, `Update(zero_Dm) - Update(zero) <= 0.03` (the program has no rule to
  execute and can only be wrong); on the orphan branch, `Update(zero_Dm) - Update(zero) <= 0` on rule
  items (the program of a deleted record can still return another record's value, the mechanism of the
  -7.8 pp); on unchanged items, `Stability(zero_Dm) - Stability(zero) >= -0.03` in S_src and S_def.
- **P-Z3** (against the stored derivation). `|Update(zero_Dm) - Update(D1sB1_source)| <= 0.10` on
  definition items in S_src and S_def.
- Before the shift (k = 0) the same contrasts are reported for the same items; the prediction for
  definition items is at least +0.30 there too (the gap is not a shift artifact).
- Consequence. P-Z1 and P-Z3 true: for the items where D helps, a stored derived item plus its binding
  is an unnecessary cost (a view does as well); the derived store earns its place on the items the view
  cannot compute. P-Z1 true and P-Z3 false in favour of D1s: storing adds something (for example the
  check against the writer's value).

### 3.7 Thinking writer (`D0think`)

Question: is the D=0 failure on definition items a failure to compute that thinking removes?

- Worlds 1000 to 1009 only.
- **P-D1.** `RepairCorrectness(D1B1_source) - RepairCorrectness(D0thinkB1_source) >= 0.30` on definition
  items in S_src and S_def (each a point estimate); the same for the mechanical variant.
- **P-D2** (validity). At most 20% of the thinking writes are unreadable (cut off by the token limit);
  otherwise the arm is reported as inconclusive and P-D1 is not judged.
- Reported, no threshold: `RepairCorrectness(D0think) - RepairCorrectness(D0)` on definition items (the
  size of the thinking gain) and the same for Update; completion tokens per write (`items.jsonl`,
  `note.think`).
- Consequence. P-D1 true: the gap is not removed by reasoning on this 9B model (the statement is about
  this model, one prompt, one budget). False: part of the D effect is aggregation failure that thinking
  repairs.

## 4. Runs, CLIs, calls

Environment. The writer and the reader are the main model; both ask the same endpoints as the published
runs, through `MEMSHIFT_ENDPOINTS` (a JSON object from model id to OpenAI-compatible base URL; several
replicas separated by `|`):

    MEMSHIFT_ENDPOINTS='{"Qwen/Qwen3.5-9B": "<replica-1 base url>|<replica-2 base url>"}'

No other endpoint is needed (no embedding service when `--no-baselines` is given). Through the project
runner, `RUN_MODULE=memshift.experiments.e1 scripts/run.sh <the arguments below>`; split seeds into
chunks of four worlds per container as before (`--seeds 1000-1003` and so on) and use distinct `--name`s.

| Run | Arguments | New model calls per world (12-month worlds 1000, 1001) |
|---|---|---|
| Keys, refresh, D0t | `--seeds 1000-1019 --branches src,def,orphan --discriminative --no-baselines --cells D0B1_source,D0B1_gold,D1B1_gold,D0B1_scope,D1B1_scope,D0B0_refresh,D1B0_refresh,D0tB0,D0tB1_source --name e1_ctrl` | writer 0 (136 to 150 distinct requests per gold cell, all cached), reader up to about 2,700 (8 cells x 324 to 362) |
| Retraction-aware | `--seeds 1000-1019 --branches src,def,orphan --discriminative --no-baselines --cells D1sB1_source,D1sB1_source_ra,D1sB0_ra --name e1_ra` | program 51 (48 items + 3 definitions that change version after S_def; fewer after the first world, because world-independent items are shared), writer 0, reader up to about 700 |
| Query-time view | `--seeds 1000-1019 --branches src,def,orphan --discriminative --no-baselines --cells zero_Dm --name e1_zerodm` | program 0 (the D1s programs), writer 0, reader up to 470 to 510 (all asked probes, k = 0 included), about 470 to 510 sandbox runs |
| Thinking writer | `--seeds 1000-1009 --branches src,def,orphan --discriminative --no-baselines --cells D0B0,D0B1_source,D0thinkB0,D0thinkB1_source --name e1_think` | thinking writer 90 to 98 (60 to 68 for B=1, 30 to 32 for B=0, 8,192 tokens at most each), writer 0, reader up to about 700 |

- The anchor cell in each run (`D0B1_source`, `D1sB1_source`, `D0B0`) is there so that the pre-shift hash
  assertion spans the new cells and an existing one; its writer and reader requests are cached.
- Reader figures are upper bounds: the asked probes after the fork (k = 1, 2, 4, 8 summed over the three
  branches); the pre-shift contexts are those of the anchors, so k = 0 reader calls are cached. The
  thinking arm sends its thinking requests only after the shift.
- Totals for 20 worlds (10 for the thinking arm), upper bounds: reader about 55,000 (keys, refresh,
  D0t) + 14,000 (retraction-aware) + 9,800 (view) + 7,000 (thinking) = about 86,000 calls of up to 4k
  prompt tokens; writer 0; program about 700 to 1,000; thinking writer about 1,000. At the rate the
  review assumed (3 to 5 GPU-hours for four cells x three branches x 20 worlds, reader calls only) this
  is on the order of 9 to 16 GPU-hours plus the thinking decode; that figure is an extrapolation, not a
  measurement.
- Reproduce the counts: `python -m memshift.experiments.e1_estimate --seeds 1000-1001 --branches
  src,def,orphan --cells <tokens>` replays the cells with a recording stand-in and prints, per world,
  distinct and new (not sent by any of the six published cells and the two D1s cells) writer, program
  and thinking requests, and the reader upper bound.

## 5. Limits that belong in the paper if these are reported

- `D0t` renders every item written after the shift in the D=1 template; D=1 uses the template only for
  items whose check failed.
- The oracle and scope keys see the benchmark's dependency and scope definitions; they bound what
  dependency tracking could give and are not implementable or are implementable only with the
  definition's machine-readable scope.
- The retraction-aware prompt is one prompt for one 9B model. Its locator fields are a design choice
  (without them every scan would declare every record); a different set changes what over-retraction
  costs. The rule "return no value when a prior read is gone" is the program's, measured and not
  enforced.
- `zero_Dm` uses the writer's own D1s programs, including the ones that fail after a retraction; a
  view built from authored rules is the ceiling and is not a cell here.
- The thinking arm is one model, one budget (8,192 tokens), no schema, and only definition items after
  the shift.
- Not built (not in the list of seven): a D=0 arm with the full in-scope records (the access control
  of review items M-03 and F3).
